The Kconfig warning indicates that `CONFIG_MAX_THREAD_BYTES` is being set, but its dependency `USERSPACE` is not enabled. The `USERSPACE` Kconfig option depends on `ARCH_HAS_USERSPACE` (which was correctly selected in the previous attempt for ARM64) and `SRAM_REGION_PERMISSIONS`. `SRAM_REGION_PERMISSIONS` in turn depends on `MMU || MPU`. The ARM64 architecture typically uses an MMU, but `config ARM64` in `arch/Kconfig` does not explicitly `select CPU_HAS_MMU`. Adding this selection will enable `MMU`, then `SRAM_REGION_PERMISSIONS`, and finally `USERSPACE`, resolving the Kconfig warning.

The runtime assertion failure in `test_sys_sem_take_multiple` is due to incorrect assertion logic after the thread priorities were reordered in the previous attempt. In the current code, `sem_take_multiple_high_prio_helper` has the highest priority (1), `sem_take_multiple_mid_prio_helper` has medium priority (2), and `sem_take_multiple_low_prio_helper` has the lowest priority (3). The assertions in the test function were not updated to correctly reflect this new priority order, leading to `low_prio_sem` being checked when `high_prio_sem` should have been incremented first. The fix involves correcting the `sys_sem_count_get` calls and `zassert_true` messages to match the actual execution order based on the assigned thread priorities.

